course: french
lesson: 4
title: Hello and goodbye: greetings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In France a greeting is not a nice extra: you say bonjour to the baker, the bus driver and the doctor before anything else, and skipping it sounds rude.
explain: Picture a school trip: Josh bursts into a bakery in Boulogne, shouts "Hiya!" and points at a cake; the baker just stares until he says "Bonjour, madame".
explain: French has a polite way and a friendly way to say hello: bonjour works with everyone, salut only with friends, family and kids your age.
explain: Goodbyes tell you when you will meet again: à demain is tomorrow, à bientôt is soon, à plus is later.
explain: Young people also have slang greetings, like wesh; it is real French, but street French, for friends only.
fact: Bonjour ! ("bon-ZHOOR") means hello or good morning and is used all day until the evening.
fact: Salut ! ("sa-LU") means hi or bye between friends, children and family; it sounds too cheeky with a teacher or shopkeeper.
fact: Au revoir ! ("oh ruh-VWAR") means goodbye; it is written as two words and works with anyone.
fact: Bonsoir ! ("bon-SWAR") means good evening and is a hello; Bonne nuit ! ("bon NWEE") means good night and is said only before bed.
fact: Ça va ? means "How are you?"; Ça va bien, merci ! means "I'm fine, thanks!"; Et toi ? means "And you?" to a friend.
fact: With the voice going up, ça va is a question; with the voice going down, it is an answer.
fact: Comment tu t'appelles ? means "What's your name?" and the answer is Je m'appelle..., "I call myself...".
fact: La bise is a light greeting with cheeks touching and a kiss in the air; two is common in Paris, while some regions do one, three or even four. A handshake, or a smile and "bonjour", is always fine if you are not sure.
fact: Coucou ! ("koo-KOO") is a warm, playful hi for family, small children and good friends; un coucou is also a cuckoo bird.
fact: À demain ! means see you tomorrow, À bientôt ! means see you soon, and À plus ! ("a PLUSS") means see you later; teens write it A+.
fact: Enchanté ! means "pleased to meet you"; a girl or woman writes Enchantée, with an extra e, but it sounds the same.
fact: Wesh is a slang hi among French teenagers that came from Arabic; Bien ou quoi ? is slang for "You alright?"
fact: Slang here: "hiya" (UK) means hi; "wicked" (Boston) means very; "grand" (Ireland) means fine; "gonnae" (Scotland) means going to; "salty" means annoyed; "skibidi talk" is kids' word for nonsense talk.
exercise: Josh walks into a French bakery. What should he say first? | Bonjour, madame ! | Salut, ma pote ! | Bonne nuit ! | Je veux un gâteau !
exercise: Until what time of day can you say bonjour? | Until the evening | Only until 9 in the morning | Only until lunch | Only on school days
exercise: Inès sends Zara a voice note: "Salut Zara !" What does salut mean here? | Hi | I salute you, soldier | Goodbye forever | Excuse me, madame
exercise: Who should you NOT greet with salut? | The shopkeeper in the bakery | Your best friend at school | Your little cousin | Your penpal of your age
exercise: How do you write goodbye in French? | Au revoir | Aurevoir | O revoir | Au revwar
exercise: Mason's cousin says Bonsoir at 7 in the evening. What does it mean? | Good evening, a hello | Good night, I'm off to bed | Good morning, nice to see you | Goodbye for ever
exercise: When do you say Bonne nuit ! ? | When someone is going to bed | When you arrive at a party at night | When you meet a teacher in the morning | When you get to school
exercise: What does Ça va ? mean? | How are you? | What's your name? | Where are you going? | Who is that?
exercise: Aoife hears Ça va, et toi ? Which reply fits? | Ça va bien, merci ! | Je m'appelle Aoife. | Bonne nuit ! | Au revoir, monsieur !
exercise: A girl asks Ellie, "Comment tu t'appelles ?" What is she asking? | What's your name? | Are you hungry? | How old are you? | Where do you live?
exercise: How should Ellie answer Comment tu t'appelles ? | Je m'appelle Ellie. | Oui, merci. | Je suis bien. | Salut, Ellie !
exercise: What is la bise? | A light cheek-to-cheek greeting with a kiss in the air | A strong handshake between two grown-ups | A wave from across the street | A hug that lifts you off the ground
exercise: Little Hugo says "Coucou !" to Olivia. What does he mean? | A friendly, playful hi | You are a silly cuckoo bird | Go away, I'm busy | Watch out, a bird!
exercise: What does À demain ! mean? | See you tomorrow! | See you in a minute! | Have a nice weekend! | Good morning!
exercise: How does a French girl write "pleased to meet you"? | Enchantée ! | Enchanté ! | Enchantes ! | Enchanter !
exercise: Rory hears a teen say "Wesh, bien ou quoi ?" What is it? | Teen slang for "Hi, you alright?" | Not French at all, made-up nonsense | A rude word you must never say | Old French from a fairy tale
exercise: Which goodbye is very casual, and written A+ in messages? | À plus ! | À demain ! | Au revoir ! | Bonne nuit !
test: Zara texts her mum, "is she saluting me lol?" What does salut mean when her penpal says it? | Hi, between friends | I salute you, like a soldier | Good evening, madam | Please be quiet
test: Tom in Yorkshire writes "Orrevwar, see ya!" in his book. What is the right spelling? | Au revoir | Orrevoir | Aurevoir | Au revwar
test: Mason goes, "Wicked weird, is bonsoir like good night?" ("Wicked" means very.) What's the answer? | No, bonsoir is a hello in the evening; good night is bonne nuit | Yes, they mean exactly the same thing, so either is fine | No, bonsoir means good morning and is used before school | Yes, but only when you say it to a grown-up
test: Aoife says "Yeah grand" in English when asked "Ça va, et toi ?" ("Grand" means fine.) What could she have said? | Ça va bien, merci ! | Au revoir, et toi ! | Je m'appelle bien. | Bonne nuit, merci !
test: Lucas tells his dad, "Mate, she went for two kisses, I nearly headbutted her!" What's the best advice? | Watch the other person and follow, or just smile and say bonjour | Always turn away, la bise is only for grown-ups in love | Always do four kisses, that's the rule everywhere | Shake hands very hard so they don't try again
test: Olivia is a bit salty: "Is he calling me a cuckoo?" ("Salty" means annoyed.) What's the truth? | No, coucou is a warm, playful hi | Yes, coucou always means "you silly bird" | Yes, but only when said to a girl | No, coucou means goodbye forever
test: Harvey says, "Ah'm gonnae say au revoir ten times, then." ("Gonnae" means going to.) What could he say instead if he meets the class tomorrow? | À demain ! | Bonsoir ! | Bonjour ! | Enchanté !
test: Sienna whispers, "Is she doing magic?" when a gran says "Enchantée !" What did the gran mean? | Pleased to meet you | I'm putting a spell on you | I'm very tired today | Goodbye, see you soon
test: Rory asks, "Is wesh even French, or is it skibidi talk?" What's the best reply? | It's real French, but slang for friends of your age | It's not French, it's English with an accent | It's the most polite word in all of French | It's only used by grandparents in the countryside
test: A cartoon hero walks into a Paris shop at 10 in the morning and says "Bonne nuit !" to the baker. What's wrong? | He should say bonjour; bonne nuit is for bedtime | Nothing, that's how you say hello in the morning | He should say salut, because bakers are friends | He should say au revoir when he comes in
test: ★★★ Which day's greetings are all right? Morning at school to the teacher; evening at a party; bedtime with Mum. | Bonjour, madame. / Bonsoir ! / Bonne nuit ! | Salut ! / Bonne nuit ! / Bonsoir ! | Bonsoir ! / Bonjour ! / Bonne nuit ! | Bonjour, madame. / Bonne nuit ! / Bonsoir !
test: ★★★ Inès writes Enchantée under her text. Tom writes Enchanté. What does this tell you? | Inès is a girl and Tom is a boy | Inès is older than Tom | Inès is much more polite than Tom | Tom forgot a letter by mistake
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the customary use of bonjour in France? | It is expected as an opening courtesy towards any person addressed during the day | It is reserved exclusively for close acquaintances and relatives | It is employed solely upon retiring for the night | It is considered impolite when addressed to shopkeepers
exam: Identify the expression appropriate when a person retires to bed. | Bonne nuit | Bonsoir | Bonjour | Salut
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the greeting salut. | It is an informal greeting suitable among friends, children and family | It is a formal greeting required when addressing officials | It is employed exclusively in written correspondence | It signifies gratitude and is the equivalent of "thank you"
exam: It is given that a female person, upon an introduction, writes the expression signifying "pleased to meet you". Determine its correct written form. | Enchantée | Enchanté | Enchantés | Enchanter
